course: dictionary
lesson: 14047
title: Mar: spoil or March
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Mar is a verb. It means to spoil or damage something, so it becomes less good or less beautiful than before. «The match was marred by rain.» The rain spoiled the match, so it was not as good as it could have been.
explain: Writers use mar in books and news. In everyday talk, people say spoil or ruin instead. «The picture was marred by a scratch.» The scratch makes the picture less lovely, but the picture is still there.
explain: Mar has a second meaning. In calendars and lists, Mar. with a full stop is short for March, the month. Context tells you which one you have: a verb in a sentence, or a month in a list of dates.
fact: Mar is a verb: mar, mars, marred, marring.
fact: «Mar» means to spoil or damage something, making it less good.
fact: «Marred» means was spoiled: «The holiday was marred by rain.»
fact: In everyday talk, people say spoil or ruin instead of mar.
fact: Mar. with a full stop, in a calendar or list, is short for March.
exercise: «The party was marred by a broken speaker.» What does marred mean here? | *Spoiled | Made louder | Cleaned | Lit up
exercise: In a calendar you see Mar. 14. What does Mar. stand for? | Mouse | *March | Mary | Market
exercise: Which everyday word is a good swap for mar when you talk? | *Spoil | Smile | Wander | Shine
test: «The stain marred her white dress.» What does marred mean in this sentence? | Dyed | Cleaned | Stretched | *Spoiled
test: Which word means to spoil or damage something? | *Mar | Mend | Praise | Polish
exam: «Rain marred the picnic, but we still had fun.» Which word could replace marred with almost the same meaning? | Cheered | *Spoiled | Helped | Filled
